#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161804 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "129940fb-a409-55bc-9852-f68f4f9ffd11" :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/id #uuid "7f92bdfc-9d59-585c-8917-9198c4a053f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9183" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n${(c)/((1 + x + y)^{3}),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x < y \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c$." :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\,\\textrm{d}y= 1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\lt y \\}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3052/Source/Images/f7c3d4d867e4cd8c83125f9b879c525e.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c > 0$, we clearly have $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$. So the first condition is satisfied.\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have\n$\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y = \\iint_{D} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y = \\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\int_{x}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{c}{(1+x+y)^3} \\;\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x= 1$.\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{∞}[∫_{x}^{∞}(c)/((1 + x + y)^{3})dy]dx &= c∫_{0}^{∞}[∫_{x}^{∞}(1 + x + y)^{-3}dy]dx \\\\\n&= c∫_{0}^{∞}[- \\frac{1}{2}(1 + x + y)^{-2}]_{y = x}^{y = ∞}dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{c}{2}∫_{0}^{∞}[(1 + x + y)^{-2}]_{y = x}^{y = ∞}dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{c}{2}∫_{0}^{∞}0 - (1 + 2x)^{-2}dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{c}{2}∫_{0}^{∞} - (1 + 2x)^{-2}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{c}{2}∫_{0}^{∞}(1 + 2x)^{-2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n> Then, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n> $\\frac{c}{2}∫_{0}^{∞}(1 + 2x)^{-2}dx|= \\frac{c}{2}[- \\frac{1}{2}(1 + 2x)^{-1}]_{0}^{∞}; =-\\frac{c}{4}[\\frac{1}{1 + 2x}]_{0}^{∞}; =-\\frac{c}{4}[0 - 1]; = \\frac{c}{4}$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{4}=1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c=4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161805 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2bcf1eb2-cf04-5dd0-bb43-682c7e9cd953" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Using a Joint PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "068714ca-5127-509c-a4e1-51374c6afbed" :question/math-academy-id "e-9184" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n${\\frac{1}{6},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 3, 0 \\le y \\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nFind $P(X > 1, Y < 1)$." :question/worked-solution "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A\\subset \\mathbb R^2$, then\n\n$$\nP\\left((X,Y)\\in A\\right) = \\iint_A f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region\n\n$$\nD = [0,3]\\times [0,2]\n$$\n\nand the event\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{X > 1, Y < 1\\right\\}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to a subset of $D$, shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3052/Source/Images/a5d3eeb749188c014bfe84991e95ddc8.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 1, Y < 1) &= ∬_{A}f(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}∬_{A}dxdy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}Area(A) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot (3 - 1) \\cdot (1 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161810 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a66952d-4e4b-5249-8f44-27d6fd198ea8" :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Rectangular Domains"}] :question/id #uuid "1ebb1eb5-e7f2-557c-aafa-e11dcb2b04e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9207" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n${c\\sin (x + y),\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < \\frac{π}{2}, 0 < y < \\frac{π}{2} \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c$." :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y= 1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0\\lt x\\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},0\\lt y \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3052/Source/Images/f9bda8492314ef0374cbb01c5b669ef2.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c > 0$, we clearly have $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$. So the first condition is satisfied.\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have\n$\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}c\\sin (x+y) \\;\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x= 1$.\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n$∫_{0}^{π/2}[∫_{0}^{π/2}c\\sin (x + y)dy]dx|= c∫_{0}^{π/2}[∫_{0}^{π/2}\\sin (x + y)dy]dx; = c∫_{0}^{π/2}[- \\cos (x + y)]_{y = 0}^{y = π/2}dx; = c∫_{0}^{π/2}(-\\cos (x + \\frac{π}{2}) + \\cos x)dx; = c∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos x - \\cos (x + \\frac{π}{2})dx$\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n$c∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos x - \\cos (x + \\frac{π}{2})dx|= c[\\sin x - \\sin (x + \\frac{π}{2})]_{0}^{π/2}; = c[(\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) - \\sin π) - (\\sin (0) - \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}))]; = c[(1 - 0) - (0 - 1)]; = c[1 - (-1)]; = 2c$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n2c=1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "129940fb-a409-55bc-9852-f68f4f9ffd11" :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f9a8ba7-45ac-5f4d-8e2e-73769a6571f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158923} {:answer/id #uuid "b47b1d29-5720-5edd-8510-f36119161686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158924} {:answer/id #uuid "17fcbe20-b3b7-51b6-8f47-bc96f7828699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158925} {:answer/id #uuid "33bb3893-f4c9-5b73-8b4e-2682304755b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158926} {:answer/id #uuid "7b98593c-c219-5567-ac8d-ce2118af18d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17fcbe20-b3b7-51b6-8f47-bc96f7828699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158925} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f205024-7464-5049-9607-a68cdb30907a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158922}] :question/id #uuid "7e6281d3-4780-5346-a3c0-14376703344d" :question/math-academy-id "q-110366" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$f(x, y) = {cxy,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le y < 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a66952d-4e4b-5249-8f44-27d6fd198ea8" :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f87a401c-76a9-5ecd-8c91-bff6b7b11fc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158929} {:answer/id #uuid "fc379996-f3d1-58ce-a9b5-5b38059578b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158930} {:answer/id #uuid "0939597e-a306-503c-b390-31e845543251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158931} {:answer/id #uuid "209bb143-574f-5c2e-997c-9556a171b07a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158932} {:answer/id #uuid "30f1613e-d580-55ab-9ddc-b9c26b17ab09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "209bb143-574f-5c2e-997c-9556a171b07a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158932} :answer-field/id #uuid "93de64f8-ad94-53d8-9f6a-0a464e19ba9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158928}] :question/id #uuid "948fa188-23c4-57d9-97ec-d87e8b50b8a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-110367" :question/problem "Let $D = [0, 2] \\cdot [0, 2]$. Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\n1. $f(x, y) = {x + y,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n2. $g(x, y) = {-xy,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n3. $h(x, y) = {\\frac{1}{4}xy,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$"}]}
